---
import Container from '../Container.astro';

/**
 * The homepage masthead, and the only place the site states its premise.
 *
 * Structure is borrowed from a magazine cover rather than a portfolio header:
 * the statement at display scale, then a hairline carrying the two standfirst
 * lines.
 *
 * There used to be a small mono nameplate above the statement, carrying the
 * page's `h1`. It was the same two words as the header wordmark directly above
 * it, so it has gone. The statement is the `h1` now, which is the better
 * heading anyway: it is what the page actually says. The header wordmark stays
 * a running head rather than a heading, so the page still has exactly one.
 *
 * That leaves the accent one job on this page: the full stop that ends the
 * statement. One blue glyph at display scale is the whole of the page's
 * colour, enough to register and too little to decorate.
 *
 * The statement's line breaks are set by hand instead of being left to the
 * browser: at display scale a bad wrap is the difference between a masthead and
 * an accident. Three spans of 13 to 14 characters hold the same shape at every
 * breakpoint, a near-square block of type, because `--text-display` clamps
 * between 48px and 132px and the measure grows at roughly the same rate.
 * Nothing constrains that block's width, deliberately: a column narrow enough
 * to be a container is a column narrow enough to rewrap the statement. The
 * explicit `text-wrap: normal` is part of that: `h1` is balanced site-wide,
 * and below ~430px the spans are wider than the measure, so balancing would
 * quietly re-break lines that are set by hand on purpose.
 *
 * The standfirst stays stacked until `lg`: at 768px a twelve-column split
 * leaves each half too narrow for `text-lead` and orphans "by habit." on its
 * own line. Its second line is broken by hand too, in three shapes: three
 * short lines below 768px, balanced automatically in the full-width band at
 * 768 to 1023px, and two lines in the half-width column from 1024px up. Every
 * automatic option left a two-word widow at one width or another.
 *
 * The `annotation` slot is the one place anything may sit beside the masthead.
 * It takes the second row of the standfirst's right-hand column, so a note
 * hangs off the same rail as the standfirst on large screens and falls in
 * below it, in reading order, when the grid collapses. It is opt-in: with the
 * slot empty the grid is exactly the two-column standfirst it always was.
 */
interface Props {}

const hasAnnotation = Astro.slots.has('annotation');
---

<section class="pt-16 md:pt-24 lg:pt-28">
  <Container>
    <h1 class="text-display text-ink [text-wrap:normal]">
      <span class="block">I build things</span>
      <span class="block">to see if they</span>
      <span class="block">
        should exist<span class="text-accent">.</span>
      </span>
    </h1>

    <div class="mt-6 grid gap-6 border-rule pt-6 md:mt-16 md:pt-8 lg:grid-cols-12 lg:gap-x-6">
      <p class="text-body text-muted md:text-balance lg:col-span-6 lg:col-start-7 lg:row-start-1 lg:text-pretty">
        A record of random products, experiments,
        <br class="md:hidden" /> side quests,
        <br class="hidden lg:inline" /> and things I learned
        <br class="md:hidden" /> along the way.
      </p>

      {hasAnnotation && (
        <div class="mt-4 lg:col-span-6 lg:col-start-7 lg:row-start-2">
          <slot name="annotation" />
        </div>
      )}
    </div>
  </Container>
</section>
